"Surah al-Kafirun was revealed in the Meccan period and consists of six verses. This surah is also called 'al-Muqashqishah'; this name means that which cures or rids one of diseases such as polytheism (shirk) and hypocrisy (nifaq). It is also referred to as the 'Surah of Worship' and the 'Surah of Sincerity'. Together with Surah al-Kawthar, they are described as 'al-Ikhlasayn' (the two surahs of sincerity). In hadiths, the virtue of this surah has been emphasized, and it has been stated that it is equivalent to a quarter of the Quran.\n\nAs for the occasion of revelation, the following incident is narrated: The notables of Quraysh came to our Prophet (peace be upon him) and proposed, 'You worship our gods, and we will worship your God; whichever is better, we will all attain it together.' The Prophet rejected this offer, saying, 'Allah forbid that I should associate partners with Allah!' and upon this, Allah Almighty revealed this surah.\n\nThe surah begins with the command, 'Say: O disbelievers!' This address is specifically directed toward those who are known in the sight of Allah as never coming to faith. Our Prophet did not utter these words of his own accord, but conveyed them by the command he received from Allah. This call is a decisive refutation of the polytheists' proposal and a warning that exposes their persistence in disbelief.\n\nIn the second and third verses, it is stated: 'I do not worship what you worship. Nor do you worship what I worship.' Here, our Prophet declares that he will never worship the idols of the polytheists and that they will never properly worship Allah. For the condition of worship is sincerity (ikhlas); worship performed by associating partners with Allah is invalid.\n\nAlthough the fourth and fifth verses appear to be a repetition, they actually point to different periods of time. The expression 'Nor am I going to worship what you have worshipped' indicates that he did not worship idols in the past either, while the expression 'Nor are you going to worship what I worship' indicates that they will not worship with sincerity in the future either. These repetitions serve to dash the hopes of those who persist in disbelief.\n\nThe sixth verse: 'For you is your religion, and for me is my religion.' This carries the meaning that their religious choices are not interfered with, but believers will not compromise on their own religion either. The verse is not a proposal for a treaty, but a rejection and a warning. For this reason, there is no abrogation (naskh) in the surah; all of it is clear and decisive (muhkam). The surah teaches believers to stay away from disbelief and hypocrisy, and to worship with sincerity.\n\nIn this section, emphasis is placed on the use of the word \"'abid\" (worshipper) in the verse as an active participle (ism al-fa'il) and on the meaning of the particle \"ma\". Although al-Kisa'i accepted that the active participle could govern a conjunctive \"ma\" (al-mawsulah) carrying a past-tense meaning, the majority of scholars reject this. Although it is mentioned in al-Kashshaf that this view of al-Kisa'i was adopted, it was considered improbable. For this reason, it is more appropriate for \"'abid\" in the first verse to convey a future meaning and in the second to convey a present meaning. However, the use of the past tense form \"you have not worshipped\" in the third verse provides a clue to considering a past-tense connotation by way of allusive indication in \"'abid\" here as well. Therefore, neither the present and future meaning nor the past meaning should be neglected. This grammatical governance occurs either by way of formal correspondence (mushakalah) with the preceding, or because \"ma\" is interpreted in the sense of worship, functioning like a masdariyyah (verbal noun) in place of an absolute object (maf'ul mutlaq). In this case, treating \"ma\" in the subsequent verses as masdariyyah and directing it toward an absolute active participle so as to encompass all three—past, present, and future—in negation is more fitting for the comprehensive inclusion (istighraq) of an indefinite noun occurring in a negative context (siyaq al-nafy), and serves as the strongest of emphatic reinforcements.\n\nHere, many subtle nuances are pointed out through the various conjugations of the verb of worship. Numerous interpretive forms emerge depending on the meanings of the particles \"ma\" and the ways the conjunctions \"waw\" connect them. The verbs carry a present-tense meaning; however, since it is not possible in Turkish to form a relative clause directly from the present and imperfect verb, expressions such as \"that which you worship / worshipped, that which I worship\" have been used in the translation. Yet this distinction is important; for while both \"that which you are worshipping\" (present) and \"that which you worshipped\" (past) are used for the addressees, only the expression \"that which I worship\" (present) is used for the portion pertaining to the Prophet, and the past is passed over silently. This serves as a reminder that the action of the Prophet that must be followed is his worship from the onset of his prophethood onward.\n\nIn the exegesis of the sixth verse, emphasis is placed on the statement \"Lakum dinukum wa liya din\" (For you is your religion, and for me is my religion). According to the majority of commentators, this is a reinforcement of the earlier statements \"I do not worship what you worship\" and \"Nor am I a worshipper of what you worship.\" That is to say, this is the proclamation of steadfastness. The Prophet's religion is the true religion of Islam, which consists of worship and obedience to Allah with tawhid (monotheism) and sincerity. There are many interpretations of this verse: First, it is a threat, like \"Do whatever you will\" (Fussilat 40/40). Second, it is a statement uttered by the Prophet to dissuade them from inviting him to polytheism. Third, it encompasses the dimensions of belief and deeds of religion: Your religion is for you, and mine is for me. Fourth, religion here means recompense; that is, the requital for your religion is upon you, and the requital for mine is upon me. Fifth, religion means supplication (du'a); your supplication is futile, while mine is accepted. Sixth, religion means custom; your custom of polytheism is for you, and my custom received through revelation is for me.\n\nAl-Razi addresses three issues. The first issue: Does this verse permit disbelief? No, because the Prophet was sent to forbid disbelief. What is meant is a threat, deterrence, or saying: 'If being destroyed is good for you, then remain upon your religion.' The second issue: The statement \"For you is your religion\" carries the meaning of restriction (takhsis, belonging exclusively to you). This points to the verses \"And that there is not for man except that [good] for which he strives\" (al-Najm 53/39) and \"No bearer of burdens will bear the burden of another\" (al-Isra 17/15). That is, everyone is responsible for their own deeds. The Prophet's duty is conveyance, and your persistence in disbelief will not harm him. Al-Razi explains this restriction as 'it belongs to you, not to me; and it belongs to me, not to you.' Abu al-Su'ud expresses it even more clearly: 'Your religion is assigned exclusively to you and does not pass over to my side; and my religion is assigned exclusively to me and does not pass over to you.'\n\nThe third issue: Al-Razi states that it is not permissible for people to use this verse as an illustrative citation (tamthil) during treaties, because the Quran was not revealed for casual quotation, but to be reflected upon and acted upon. However, al-Alusi objects to this; quotation (iqtibas) is permissible, and there are examples of it in the words of the Prophet, the Companions, and scholars. Yet it must not be forgotten that quoting is not appropriate everywhere, but only in contexts that are suitable and not contrary to reverence. Whether this verse is abrogated (mansukh) has also been debated. Qadi al-Baydawi says that there is neither permission for disbelief nor prohibition of jihad here; its abrogation by the verse of fighting could only be in question if it were interpreted as a treaty. The fact that this surah is followed by Surah al-Nasr indicates that this is not of the category of the abrogated, but rather of the nature of struggle (mujahadah)."